 "question": "Was McVay to blame, or was he the Navy's scapegoat?",
 "summary": "In July 1945 the heavy cruiser USS Indianapolis carried the uranium and parts of Little Boy, the first atomic bomb used in war, to Tinian. Four days after the delivery, on 30 July, the Japanese submarine I-58 under Mochitsura Hashimoto sank her in 12 minutes. About 300 of her 1,195 men went down with the ship; the rest were left in the Philippine Sea for almost four days, because nobody reported her missing. Only 316 survived thirst, exposure and sharks. Her captain, Charles B. McVay III, became the only US Navy captain court-martialed for losing his ship in combat in World War II; Congress exonerated him in 2000.",
