  {
   "title": "The Secret Cargo",
   "mode": "people",
   "sentences": [
    "Her captain was Charles B. McVay III, the son of an admiral, who had commanded her since November 1944.",
    "Earlier in the war, he had been awarded the Silver Star for courage under fire.",
    "Now his orders were to carry a critical shipment to Tinian Island.",
    "In the hold were the non-nuclear parts of Little Boy, the first atomic bomb used in combat.",
    "The uranium itself sat in a lead-lined steel container, bolted and chained to the floor of McVay's own quarters.",
    "Two men from the Manhattan Project travelled with it incognito, Major Robert Furman and Dr. James Nolan.",
    "Nolan was seasick for much of the journey, and used it as an excuse to check on the container in secret.",
    "On 16 July, she left San Francisco, within hours of the Trinity test in New Mexico.",
    "She raced to Pearl Harbor in a record 74 and a half hours, and then raced on unaccompanied.",
    "On 26 July, she delivered her cargo to Tinian.",
    "That cargo became the bomb dropped on Hiroshima on the morning of 6 August.",
    "Among the messages the ground crews wrote on it, one read: Greetings to the Emperor from the men of the Indianapolis.",
    "By then, most of the men it named were already dead.",
    "Later, General Groves learned that she had been a very poor choice for the job.",
    "She had no underwater sound equipment, and was so designed that a single torpedo could sink her quickly."
   ],
