  {
   "title": "Nobody Is Coming",
   "mode": "people",
   "sentences": [
    "The Navy's headquarters on Guam and on Leyte plotted the positions of every ship on large boards.",
    "But ships as large as Indianapolis were assumed to arrive on time, so their positions were predictions, not reports.",
    "On 31 July, when she should have reached Leyte, she was simply taken off the board on Guam.",
    "On Leyte, she was even recorded as having arrived.",
    "At Tacloban, Lieutenant Stuart Gibson knew at once that she had not come in.",
    "He did not look into it, and made no report to his superiors.",
    "Expected ships often failed to arrive, and no rule said he had to report it.",
    "Her distress calls had not simply vanished, either.",
    "Records declassified years later showed that three stations received them.",
    "One commander was drunk, another had ordered his men not to disturb him, and a third thought it was a Japanese trap.",
    "None of them acted.",
    "And so, out in the Philippine Sea, hundreds of men waited for a rescue that nobody had ordered."
   ],
