  {
   "title": "The Scapegoat",
   "mode": "people",
   "sentences": [
    "Many came to believe that the Navy needed someone to blame.",
    "A Navy Court of Inquiry recommended a court-martial, but Admiral Nimitz disagreed, and gave McVay only a letter of reprimand.",
    "Admiral Ernest King overturned Nimitz's decision.",
    "In November 1945, McVay was court-martialed, and convicted of hazarding his ship by failing to zigzag.",
    "Knowledge of the Japanese submarines in the area was withheld from the court.",
    "And the prosecution called a surprising witness: Mochitsura Hashimoto, the man who had sunk his ship.",
    "Hashimoto testified that zigzagging would not have made a difference, and that he would have sunk her anyway.",
    "American submarine experts said much the same, and still the court held McVay responsible.",
    "About 380 ships of the Navy were lost in combat in the war, and he was the only captain court-martialed for the loss of his ship.",
    "Nimitz later had the sentence remitted, and McVay retired in 1949 as a rear admiral.",
    "But some families of the men who died blamed him.",
    "One piece of mail read: Merry Christmas! Our family's holiday would be a lot merrier if you hadn't killed my son.",
    "In 1968, at the age of 70, McVay took his own life.",
    "Decades later, a sixth-grade student named Hunter Scott chose the sinking for a class history project.",
    "His research, and the survivors who testified before the Senate, brought the case back.",
    "In October 2000, Congress passed a resolution that McVay was exonerated for the loss of Indianapolis.",
    "Hashimoto, who had become a Shinto priest, died five days before.",
    "In 2017, a search team financed by Paul Allen found the wreck, 18,000 feet down in the Philippine Sea.",
    "She lies there still, with the men who never left her.",
    "So was McVay a captain who failed his crew, or a scapegoat for the Navy's mistakes? Tell me what you think in the comments."
   ],
